## Design principles (apply throughout)
- Four layers: (1) SQL/dbt shapes data upstream into tables prefixed `rpt_`; (2) a YAML spec selects, filters, sorts, and arranges columns from those tables; (3) the engine renders and reconciles; (4) coding agents author specs and extend the engine.
- The spec must NOT be able to join, pivot, aggregate, or calculate. Filters are limited to equality, IN, and gte/lte/gt/lt on declared parameters. If something needs more, it belongs in a new rpt_ table, and you say so.
- One repo, many reports: shared package `reportkit/`, one folder per report at `reports/<id>/` containing `spec.yaml` and `commentary/`.
- Controls are enforced in code and tests, not just described in docs.
- Do not invent business values. Thresholds, wording, and colors in examples are placeholders marked `# TODO(policy)`.
- Use only: python-pptx, pydantic v2, pandas, pyyaml, pytest, and (optional) google-cloud-bigquery.

## Example spec (match this shape)
id: pd_monitoring_quarterly
title: Commercial PD Model — Quarterly Performance Monitoring
owner: Credit Risk Analytics
params:
  as_of: {type: date, required: true}
  portfolio: {type: str, default: Commercial}
datasets:
  seg:
    mart: rpt_pd_monitoring
    key: segment
    filters: {as_of_date: "{as_of}", portfolio: "{portfolio}"}
  trend:
    mart: rpt_pd_monitoring
    filters: {portfolio: "{portfolio}", as_of_date: {lte: "{as_of}"}}
    order_by: as_of_date          # "-col" = descending
formats: {gini: dec3, psi: dec3, predicted_dr: pct2, observed_dr: pct2, n_obligors: int}
thresholds:                       # TODO(policy)
  gini: {label: Gini, direction: higher_better, green: 0.50, amber: 0.40}
  psi:
    label: PSI
    direction: lower_better
    green: 0.10
    amber: 0.25
    by: {segment: {CRE Investor: {green: 0.15}}}
slides:
  - title: Discrimination by Segment
    exhibits:
      - {type: table, title: Discrimination, data: seg, key: segment, columns: [n_obligors, gini], rag: [gini]}
  - title: Population Stability
    layout: two_up
    exhibits:
      - {type: bar, title: PSI by segment, data: seg, category: segment, values: [psi], rag: [psi]}
      - {type: line, title: PSI trend, data: trend, x: as_of_date, y: psi, series: segment}
  - title: Commentary
    layout: commentary
    exhibits: [{type: commentary, title: Commentary}]
commentary:
  red: "{label} for {key} is Red at {value}, breaching the Amber threshold of {amber}."
  amber: "{label} for {key} is Amber at {value} against a Green threshold of {green}."
  all_green: "All monitored metrics are within Green thresholds."
outputs: [pptx, html]

## Stage 1 — Spec, data, controls
- Pydantic models for the spec. Validate that table names start with `rpt_` and all table/column names are plain identifiers. Reject unknown filter operators, undeclared params, unknown format names, and exhibits referencing undefined datasets. Check that the exhibit count matches the slide layout.
- Named formats in one module (str, int, dec2, dec3, pct1, pct2, bps, usd_mm, usd_k), each with a Python text format and an Excel number format. bps scales by 10,000, and chart data must be scaled the same way the labels are.
- RAG logic plus the Threshold model in their own module that does not import the spec module (avoids a circular import). Support per-row overrides via `by`.
- Two data backends chosen by env var REPORTKIT_BACKEND: `fixtures` (default; reads fixtures/<mart>.csv with synthetic data) and `bigquery` (read-only, fully parameterized queries, never string-built SQL). Params are substituted only as a whole filter value like "{as_of}", never by partial string templating.
- Provenance: params, source table per dataset, row counts, data hash, spec hash, git SHA, UTC run time.
- Include synthetic fixture data covering four quarters and five segments.
Tests: validators reject bad table names, injection-looking columns, and disallowed operators; RAG boundaries are exact at the green/amber edges; segment overrides work.

## Stage 2 — Exhibits, PowerPoint, reconciliation
- Exhibit registry: each type is one module with a pydantic class implementing render_pptx, render_html, and reconcile. Ship table, bar, column, line (time series, one line per series value), and commentary.
- Native PowerPoint objects only; never images of charts. Style (colors, fonts, sizes, layout boxes, master layout names) comes from one style module. Use a branded master from templates/ if present. Otherwise use the default master resized to 16:9 and reposition title placeholders (the default master assumes 4:3). RAG cells: dark text on amber, white on red and green.
- Provenance goes in every slide's notes and in a footer on every content slide.
- Reconciliation on every build: every table cell's text and every chart series value is compared to source; the build exits non-zero on any mismatch or on extra/missing exhibits.
Tests: the example report builds; tampering with one table cell is caught; replacing one chart's data is caught.

## Stage 3 — Facts, commentary, CLI
- Facts: for each dataset with a `key` and each threshold metric, emit metric, label, key, formatted value, status, and the applicable green/amber thresholds (formatted).
- Default commentary: deterministic sentences from the spec templates, Red before Amber, then ordered by metric and key.
- Optional draft at reports/<id>/commentary/<as_of>.md with YAML frontmatter. Every numeric token in the draft must appear in the formatted facts, or the build fails. Exempt years, dates, and quarter labels. The slide shows "DRAFT — requires analyst sign-off" until a human sets `signed_off_by`; nothing in the code ever sets it.
- CLI: `reportkit build|facts|check reports/<id> -p key=value [--out dir]`.
Tests: a grounded draft passes; a draft containing an invented number fails.

## Stage 4 — Prove it generalizes
- Add a second report: monthly RAROC committee pack. Two rpt_ tables (by segment; top clients), usd_mm/pct1/bps formats, a top-8 list sorted by exposure descending, a RAROC-vs-hurdle column chart with RAG, and a two_up layout. Include synthetic fixtures.
- Minimal HTML renderer driven by the same spec.
- Give me commands to render both decks to images with LibreOffice (soffice) so I can check layout, and list the specific defects I should look for (overflow, overlap, unreadable labels, footer collisions). Fix any issues I report in the engine, not the outputs.
- List anything the spec could not express cleanly.

## Stage 5 — Docs for humans and agents
- STYLE.md: visual rules, number conventions, and commentary rules (sentence pattern; no causes unless they're in the facts, so use "Question for review:" instead; never soften a breach; no boilerplate that would read the same regardless of the numbers).
- AGENTS.md: only the always-true rules, under 40 lines. .agents/skills/ gets one procedure each for new-report, add-exhibit, draft-commentary, and validate-report, each with a "use when…" description. Don't repeat AGENTS.md inside skills.
- dbt_examples/ showing what rpt_ models look like (illustration only; the engine must not depend on dbt).
- README with a quick start.
- DESIGN.md for Model Risk Management (1–2 pages): the controls, where each is enforced in code, what the tests prove, and what is explicitly out of scope.

